<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<!--
  The application manifest for the `dig-app` shell.

  Its whole job is to make this process's DPI awareness a BUILD-TIME FACT rather than a race.

  Windows decides a process's DPI awareness once, at the first call that sets it, and thereafter
  ignores every later attempt. Before this manifest existed the first such call was made by whichever
  event loop happened to be constructed first: tao sets PER_MONITOR_AWARE_V2 from `EventLoop::new`
  (tao 0.30.8 `platform_impl/windows/event_loop.rs:171` -> `dpi.rs:20 become_dpi_aware`, behind a
  `Once`), and winit does the same from its own. So awareness depended on which surface the user
  reached first, and a headless build (no default features) — which constructs no loop at all — was
  never aware.

  That is worse than being unaware, because the hand-built consent windows COMPENSATE for awareness:
  they read the monitor's real DPI and scale themselves (`Win32_UI_HiDpi`, dig_ecosystem#1832). Under
  a process that turned out unaware, Windows also scales them, and the two multiply. Declaring the
  awareness here removes the order-dependence entirely: it is set by the loader, before any of our
  code runs, in every build and on every path.

  Nothing else is declared beyond the two things a manifest must state to be well-formed and honest.
  In particular there is NO <compatibility> block: this app reads no OS version, and a supportedOS
  list is a claim that would then have to be maintained.

  Every comment in this file avoids a double hyphen. XML forbids one inside a comment, and mt.exe
  rejects the entire manifest for it with a parse error that names no line and no column.
-->
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">
  <assemblyIdentity type="win32" name="net.dig.dig-app" version="1.0.0.0" />

  <!--
    asInvoker, stated explicitly rather than left to the linker's default.

    dig-app is a per-user identity agent that guards custody actions behind its own consent windows.
    It must never run elevated: an elevated process cannot be sent input by the unelevated desktop it
    lives on, and asking a user for admin to run a tray app trains them to grant it. Pinned by
    `tests/windows_manifest.rs`.
  -->
  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <security>
      <requestedPrivileges>
        <requestedExecutionLevel level="asInvoker" uiAccess="false" />
      </requestedPrivileges>
    </security>
  </trustInfo>

  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <!--
        Per-monitor v2 — the same awareness tao would have set, now set unconditionally. v2 is what
        the consent windows are written against: it scales the non-client area and dialog fonts with
        the monitor, so a window dragged between a 100% and a 200% display stays the size it was
        drawn at.
      -->
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">PerMonitorV2</dpiAwareness>
      <!--
        The Windows 8.1 spelling of the same intent, for hosts older than the 1703 release that
        introduced v2. Windows reads whichever it understands; on 1703+ the element above wins.
      -->
      <dpiAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2005/WindowsSettings">true/pm</dpiAware>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
</assembly>
